<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrios Angelos Doukas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1244">1244</a>–<a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Demetrius Angelus Ducas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrios Angelos Doukas was the last independent ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>. He succeeded his brother John in 1244 with the title of despot, but his unpopular rule alienated the city's nobles, who conspired with the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. In 1246 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> took Thessalonica without a fight and annexed it, and Demetrios was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Demetrios was born around 1220, the younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> and Maria Petraliphaina, and so a great-great-grandson of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>. Unlike most of his relatives, who avoided the surname Angelos because of the disastrous reigns of the Angelid emperors, he used the name Angelos Doukas in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> notice dated 25 September 1244.</p><p>His father had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> from 1215 and, after taking the Latin Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1224, founded the Empire of Thessalonica as a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>. Theodore was defeated and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Battle of Klokotnitsa</a> in 1230 and later blinded. On his release he overthrew his brother Manuel in Thessalonica and installed his eldest son John as emperor, but in 1244 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> forced John to accept Nicaean overlordship and take the lesser title of Despot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On John's death in 1244 Demetrios succeeded him, while Theodore oversaw affairs from Vodena. An embassy announced the succession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, as the terms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> required, and Vatatzes confirmed it. Young and dissolute, Demetrios inspired little loyalty among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>'s leading families, who came to prefer rule by Nicaea, by then the strongest of the Byzantine successor states.</p><p>In 1246 Vatatzes took much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and most of Macedonia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in a three-month campaign. At Melenikon an envoy of the Thessalonian conspirators offered to deliver the city in return for a guarantee of their privileges, which Vatatzes granted. Demetrios twice refused to come and pay homage, and a few days after the Nicaean army appeared, its supporters opened a gate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Demetrios fled to the citadel but was persuaded to yield by his sister Irene, who had won clemency for him from Vatatzes. Thessalonica was absorbed into the Nicaean state under the governor Andronikos Palaiologos. Demetrios was exiled to the fortress of Lentiana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, where he probably died at an unknown date. His father, isolated at Vodena, apparently took no part in these events.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demetrios Angelos Doukas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1246 Vatatzes took Thessalonica from John's brother <b>Demetrios</b>, annexing it to the Empire of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a></li></ul></section>
